update lr_explanation_steps t set body=v.nb, label=case when v.nl='' then t.label else v.nl end from (values
('49a6cf2d-3ad4-48a5-805a-d29fd8627b5f'::uuid,'1689a34952a2fd21858ef29706c6c9b0','The blank comes after "since", so it is the conclusion, and everything before it is the evidence. Let''s line up what happens to the workers.

Mass production lowered prices by using fewer workers, many of whom needed little training. Lower prices let workers buy goods they could not afford before. That''s a gain.

Then the pivot: "But since" jobs needing little training are more vulnerable to elimination. That''s a cost, and it lands on exactly the kind of job mass production relies on.

So one cause, mass production, does two things to the same people. It raises what they can buy, and it makes their jobs less secure. The "But" tells us the conclusion should bring in the downside without erasing the upside.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('61d0699b-ae2f-4f00-8933-8c2be2ded79f'::uuid,'8b9a0c23e6c20db17fc413cb809448b1','Predict a modest answer that ties the two together: the same thing that boosted workers'' buying power also left their jobs more exposed. Be wary of answers that compare groups the stimulus never compares, bring in new topics such as profits, or turn a vulnerability into job losses that have already happened.','Goal','Goal'),
('90bd8980-9a76-4618-9087-855534bd9189'::uuid,'fef9249ddaf26ac57bea76be29cbb9f4','Start with the structure. "Although" opens a concession: a single application gives short-term gains. The main point will push against that. "We should conclude that" marks the blank as the conclusion, and the sentence just before it, that long-term use gradually depresses yield, is the evidence driving it.

Now read that evidence closely. Yields go up at first, then slide down "from this initially elevated level". It doesn''t say yields fall below where they started. It says nothing about costs, the environment, or the pests themselves. So the safe conclusion is about a payoff that shrinks over time, not one that disappears or does harm.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('f498eaa0-c6c2-4cbc-a1ce-0f94331a4594'::uuid,'6cbf8f1d8eb38321d38d2096484fce99','Predict something like this: the benefit of these pesticides fades with continued use. On a completion like this, reject anything stronger than a fading benefit (a net loss, an out-of-control pest problem) and anything that brings in a topic the stimulus never raised (the environment, the instructions on the label).','Goal','Goal'),
('415fba4f-484e-4b5c-9ee4-d1a76847e01f'::uuid,'f30b129479cf8cadaf3adead653c531d','  - *Contrast:* mu mesons (MMs) generated by cosmic rays just outside Earth''s atmosphere travel at speeds approaching the speed of light, **whereas** MMs generated in the lab are nearly at rest.
  - *Comparison:* a lab MM typically decays in much less time than it takes an MM to travel from just outside the atmosphere to our detector on Earth.
  - *Conditional:* if fast MMs typically decay as fast as lab MMs, then we should detect only about one one-hundredth of the number **we actually do detect.**','Given','Given'),
('999a1eb1-7e4b-4d8e-84b2-85a83ef89eef'::uuid,'8bdcf002c72ee1e62ecde6af54a3c5d5','Let''s call mu mesons MMs. The fast ones are made just outside the atmosphere and travel to Earth at nearly the speed of light. The lab ones are nearly at rest, and they typically decay in much less time than the trip from the upper atmosphere to our detector takes.

The fourth sentence does the real work. It''s a conditional:

fast MMs decay as fast as lab MMs → we detect about 1/100 of the number we actually detect

Read the right side carefully. We can''t detect one one-hundredth of the number we actually detect. We detect the number we actually detect. So the right side is false, and when the right side of a conditional is false, the left side has to be false too. That''s the contrapositive:

NOT detect 1/100 of the actual number → fast MMs do NOT decay as fast as lab MMs

So the fast MMs don''t decay as fast as the lab ones. The blank is about "mu mesons moving at speeds near the speed of light", which are the fast ones. The sentence should finish by saying they decay more slowly than the MMs at rest.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('3e43ec14-0387-4b38-bfd3-38d94d029a21'::uuid,'5747a9737e0171967f8822b826b546fe','Look past the physics words and the prediction is short: MMs moving near the speed of light decay more slowly than MMs at rest in the lab. An answer about travel time, detection equipment, how many MMs there are, or where they come from leaves the conditional untouched.','Goal','Goal'),
('d1e3b047-7252-426f-b3f0-72cfadb06b9f'::uuid,'d19c85c61a09c2cba1e294926d00571d','Three facts about superstring theory. It purports to explain the nature and existence of gravity, which more established theories don''t even try to do. Testing it would take a particle accelerator 100 trillion kilometers long. And it has had no success explaining why gravity is as strong as it is, neither stronger nor weaker.

The second and third facts are both called a "problem". That word carries a judgment, so ask why. Why would failing to explain gravity''s strength count against this theory? Because the theory claims to explain gravity. A theory that sets out to explain a force and can''t say why the force has the strength it has is falling short of its own job.

Notice what the stimulus does not give us: anything about what the established theories have achieved, whether superstring theory is true, or how hard the theorists have tried.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('ba9171cd-c81d-4217-9c6b-7ecfc7d213c2'::uuid,'5b1bc959c4c2b07c36db20d64f2a6d2f','There''s no conditional chain to link, so we want the answer that rests on what the stimulus says or clearly implies. The best candidate is the standard behind the word "problem": a theory meant to explain a force falls short if it can''t explain that force''s strength. Be strict with answers about other theories, about truth, or about effort, since the stimulus says little or nothing on those.','Goal','Goal'),
('1e8e2fc0-11a1-4b69-8992-84747db42253'::uuid,'904dc4f9127918646128284f582be7b8','Cerrato reports two claims from economists. First, part-time workers are much more likely to be dissatisfied with their jobs than full-time workers. Second, part-time workers threaten to take jobs from full-time employees. Then the pivot, "But": job efficiency goes with job satisfaction, so companies are unlikely to replace satisfied employees with dissatisfied ones.

Put the pieces together. If part-timers are the dissatisfied ones, and companies won''t swap satisfied workers for dissatisfied ones, then companies won''t swap full-timers for those part-timers. That undercuts the second claim. Cerrato is turning the economists'' first claim against their second: the two don''t fit together.

Notice that Cerrato never says the first claim is true, only that economists argue it. So the safest completion is tied to it: to the extent part-timers are dissatisfied, they aren''t a threat to full-time jobs. Predict an answer saying dissatisfied part-time workers are unlikely to take full-timers'' jobs.','Evaluate / Goal','Evaluate / Goal'),
('b6179904-d450-4140-a885-cb87c7173964'::uuid,'617bae7c4f722556d6a5823aae04c92d','Three statements, and the last one pivots.

1. Comparison: dark honey tends to have a higher antioxidant content than light-colored honey.

2. Rule: the most healthful strains of honey are all unusually high in antioxidants.
most healthful strain → unusually high in antioxidants

3. Pivot ("However"): certain strains from bees harvesting primarily sage nectar are among the most healthful strains, even though they are among the lightest-colored.','Information','Information'),
('147da0b5-3a6b-44e8-8a93-c202fd85f11c'::uuid,'ac39d50d8dc170f9e06f38ff0aabc543','The first statement tempts us to link color and health: dark honey has more antioxidants, the healthiest honey has lots of antioxidants, so maybe the healthiest honey is dark. The "However" stops that. Some of the most healthful strains are among the lightest.

Now look for a fact that triggers a rule. Statement 3 tells us certain sage-nectar strains are among the most healthful. Statement 2 says every one of the most healthful strains is unusually high in antioxidants. Put them together:

certain sage-nectar strains → most healthful → unusually high in antioxidants

So those sage-nectar strains are unusually high in antioxidants, even though they are light-colored. That''s provable. Notice also what statement 1 doesn''t do: "tends to" is only a trend, so a light honey with lots of antioxidants doesn''t contradict it.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('f1aab2c1-3405-4b63-8310-541591d03bb9'::uuid,'62df54a15a4f3e946a427b582419ae67','Look for the answer that combines statements 2 and 3: some sage-nectar honey is unusually high in antioxidants. Avoid answers that reverse the dark-versus-light trend, or that make claims about most plants or about the single highest honey, which nothing here measures.','Goal','Goal'),
('e86242f3-17e6-401e-8a9e-1e92b3ddd0ee'::uuid,'4df57f4fc3a5054c9532dbfefdfe5e17','Predict that the strategy backfires: by giving up traditions that are each essential to the culture''s identity, the culture loses what makes it that culture. An answer can go further than calling the strategy ineffective here, because "each" tradition is essential, so losing many of them means losing the identity itself. Reject any answer that agrees with the anthropologists or carves out cultures where the strategy works.','Goal','Goal'),
('1022806f-ccbe-4ddc-bce5-e4e2b7094421'::uuid,'afa26c5d44c275efaf4cdf0c4a2574b4','In the past, infants who weren''t breast-fed were fed cow''s milk.
Doctors then advised boiling that milk to sterilize it and prevent gastrointestinal infections potentially fatal to infants.
Once the advice was widely followed, scurvy (caused by vitamin C deficiency) rose alarmingly among infants.
Breast-fed infants did not get scurvy.','Information','Information'),
('70b5db2e-ad06-426b-865b-d18a2658ac18'::uuid,'f6c023b7b4e2329a769d251b50041d9b','Start with the timing. Before the advice, infants who weren''t breast-fed drank cow''s milk. Once boiling became widespread, scurvy rose sharply. Scurvy comes from a lack of vitamin C. Breast-fed infants, who never got the boiled milk, didn''t get scurvy.

So one difference (boiled cow''s milk versus mother''s milk) lines up with another difference (scurvy versus no scurvy), and we''re told what causes scurvy. The simplest way to connect them: infants on boiled cow''s milk weren''t getting enough vitamin C, and breast-fed infants were. The rise came right after boiling caught on, which suggests the boiling cut the vitamin C, but we don''t need the mechanism. We only need the comparison.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('d704cfb4-caee-401d-b748-0ee675cad940'::uuid,'6e8fb0c0269c6f2a66120b8f4ebd3940','This is a reasonable inference, not a certainty, and that''s fine on Most Strongly Supported. Predict an answer saying boiled cow''s milk gave infants less vitamin C than mother''s milk. Watch for answers that add a frequency, a state of knowledge, or a proportion the passage never mentions.','Goal','Goal')
) v(id,ob,nb,ol,nl) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;